          mainEntity: [
          {
                    "@type": "Question",
                    "name": "How long does a rebrand take in Pakistan?",
                    "acceptedAnswer": {
                              "@type": "Answer",
                              "text": "Most rebrands take about 8 to 14 weeks from strategy sign-off to launch day, followed by a 30-day monitoring period. Pre-launch work takes 2 to 4 weeks, asset production 3 to 6 weeks and launch day itself one day. Multi-branch businesses with heavy print stock should plan toward the longer end."
                    }
          },
          {
                    "@type": "Question",
                    "name": "How much does a rebrand cost for a small business in Pakistan?",
                    "acceptedAnswer": {
                              "@type": "Answer",
                              "text": "Cost depends on scope, the number of touchpoints and print volume. A visual refresh costs less than a full rebrand with a new name, website and signage. Print and signage replacement often form the largest variable cost. Ask for an itemised quote that separates strategy, design, website, redirects and print."
                    }
          },
          {
                    "@type": "Question",
                    "name": "Should I rebrand all at once or in phases?",
                    "acceptedAnswer": {
                              "@type": "Answer",
                              "text": "Rebrand all at once when you have one location, few print items and an online-first customer base. Choose phases when you have several branches, heavy print stock or large signage. Whichever approach you pick, update Google Business Profile, WhatsApp and social profiles together so customers never see two brands online."
                    }
          },
          {
                    "@type": "Question",
                    "name": "Will a rebrand hurt my SEO?",
                    "acceptedAnswer": {
                              "@type": "Answer",
                              "text": "It can if redirects are skipped. Use permanent 301 redirects from every old page to its new equivalent, update the sitemap and internal links, and keep redirects for at least a year. If the domain itself changes, also use the Change of Address tool in Google Search Console. Expect temporary ranking movement."
                    }
          },
          {
                    "@type": "Question",
                    "name": "How do I tell customers about a rebrand?",
                    "acceptedAnswer": {
                              "@type": "Answer",
                              "text": "Announce before the change appears. Use WhatsApp broadcast lists, social posts, email and a note with each order. Say what is changing, what stays the same and why customers benefit. Show the old and new brands side by side for the first weeks so customers recognise the business immediately."
                    }
          },
          {
                    "@type": "Question",
                    "name": "Who needs a rebranding checklist?",
                    "acceptedAnswer": {
                              "@type": "Answer",
                              "text": "Any business that has decided to change its name, identity or positioning needs one, especially businesses with several branches, print stock, online listings or many customers who know the old brand. A checklist helps small teams avoid missed touchpoints because brand assets are spread across people and platforms."
                    }
          },
          {
                    "@type": "Question",
                    "name": "What are the risks of rebranding?",
                    "acceptedAnswer": {
                              "@type": "Answer",
                              "text": "The main risks are lost customer recognition, mixed branding that confuses customers, broken links and lost search visibility, wasted print stock and team confusion. Reduce risk with an asset inventory, a launch owner, redirects, a clear announcement and a transition period that shows the old and new brands together."
                    }
          },
          {
                    "@type": "Question",
                    "name": "What should I do with old packaging, signage and documents?",
                    "acceptedAnswer": {
                              "@type": "Answer",
                              "text": "Use up existing packaging stock if it carries no outdated claims, replace signage on a planned schedule and retire old documents on a fixed date. Keep a record of every item and its replacement date. Avoid destroying useful stock early, but set an end date so mixed branding does not drag on."
                    }
          }
]
